{"ts": "2026-09-01T22:41:13Z", "task_id": "TASK-7003B", "attempt": 2, "verdict": "CHANGES_REQUESTED", "failed_tests": 2, "real_execution": true, "applied_files": 1, "feedback": [{"file": "src/data/market_data.py", "line": 89, "issue": "tests/test_market_data.py::test_cache_hit_skips_second_download and tests/test_market_data.py::test_cache_miss_for_different_ticker fail with a TypeError originating at this store call: frame.reset_index().to_dict(orient='list') leaves pd.Timestamp objects in the index-column list (a DatetimeIndex surfaces as the 'Date' or 'index' column), and a contract-compliant Cache.set serializes with plain json.dumps, raising 'Object of type Timestamp is not JSON serializable'. The payload is therefore not actually JSON-safe, so the first storing fetch crashes before any cache-hit/miss behavior can be exercised.", "remediation": "Make the stored payload truly JSON-serializable before cache.set, e.g. staged = frame.reset_index(); idx = staged.columns[0]; if pd.api.types.is_datetime64_any_dtype(staged[idx]): staged[idx] = staged[idx].astype(str); then cache.set(cache_key, staged.to_dict(orient='list')). The existing hit path (_restore_datetime_index with pd.to_datetime) already restores the DatetimeIndex from ISO strings, so hit-path fidelity is preserved. Confirm against the full traceback (pytest tests/test_market_data.py::test_cache_hit_skips_second_download -q) and the set implementation in src/data/cache.py; if the traceback shows a different TypeError source, fix that instead while keeping the cache.get(cache_key) / cache.set(key, payload) call shapes unchanged."}, {"file": "src/data/market_data.py", "line": 88, "issue": "Any failure inside cache.set propagates out of fetch_ohlcv as a raw TypeError instead of the module's DataError contract, which is exactly what surfaces in the two failing tests. This must not be fixed by swallowing the exception: the cache-hit test requires the store to genuinely succeed (a silently failed store would make the second call re-download and break the zero-additional-download assertion).", "remediation": "Fix the serialization of the stored payload per the line-89 note so cache.set succeeds, keep DataError strictly for None/empty downloads per the spec, and iterate with pytest tests/test_market_data.py -q until the file is fully green."}]}
{"ts": "2026-09-01T22:52:43Z", "task_id": "TASK-7003B", "attempt": 2, "verdict": "CHANGES_REQUESTED", "failed_tests": 2, "real_execution": true, "applied_files": 1, "feedback": [{"file": "src/data/market_data.py", "line": 110, "issue": "Acceptance gate not met: tests/test_market_data.py::test_cache_hit_skips_second_download and tests/test_market_data.py::test_cache_miss_for_different_ticker both fail with a TypeError. The payload passed to Cache.set, frame.reset_index().to_dict(orient='list'), is not actually JSON-serializable in practice: the reset DatetimeIndex column contains pandas Timestamp/datetime objects (and numeric columns may hold numpy scalars depending on the pandas version). Per the documented Cache contract, set() raises TypeError for non-JSON-serializable values, so the first store on a miss blows up before the cache-hit path can ever be exercised.", "remediation": "Run pytest tests/test_market_data.py::test_cache_hit_skips_second_download -x to capture the exact traceback and confirm it originates in Cache.set. Then store a genuinely JSON-safe payload: coerce the reset index column to ISO-8601 strings (e.g., .astype(str) or .map(datetime.isoformat)) and ensure native scalar types before calling cache.set, while keeping _rebuild_frame's pd.to_datetime(...) restoration so cache hits rebuild an identical DatetimeIndex. Cross-check src/data/cache.py's set() serializer to confirm which types it rejects, then re-run tests/test_market_data.py until fully green (0 failures, no skips for this file)."}]}
